    "evidence_text": "This raises an important question: Can we collect a large benchmark dataset that can last longer?\n\nThe speed with which benchmarks become obsolete raises another important question: are current NLU models genuinely as good as their high performance on benchmarks suggests?\n\nWe propose an iterative, adversarial human-and-model-in-the-loop solution for NLU dataset collection that addresses both benchmark longevity and robustness issues.\n\nIn the first stage, human annotators devise examples that our current best models cannot determine the correct label for.\n\nThese resulting hard examples\u2014which should expose additional model weaknesses\u2014can be added to the training set and used to train a\n\nstate-of-the-art performance on a variety of popular NLI benchmarks.\n\nThe ANLI dataset is available at github.com/facebookresearch/anli/.\n\nA demo of the annotation procedure can be viewed at adversarialnli.com.\n\nDataset collection\tThe primary aim of this work is to create a new large-scale NLI benchmark on which current state-of-the-art models fail.\n\nThis constitutes a new target for the field to work towards, and can elucidate model capabilities and limitations.\n\nAs noted, however, static benchmarks do not last very long these days.\n\nIf continuously deployed, the data collection procedure we introduce here can pose a dynamic challenge that allows for never-ending learning.\n\nDataset collection ::: HAMLET\tTo paraphrase the great bard , there is something rotten in the state of the art.\n\nIn our setup, our starting point is a base model, trained on NLI data.\n\nRather\n\nAfter\n\nExamples from each round of dataset collection are provided in Table TABREF2.\n\nDataset statistics\tThe dataset statistics can be found in Table TABREF7.\n\nFor each round, we report the model error rate, both on verified and unverified examples.\n\nThe\n\ndesign.\n\nFor rounds 2 and 3, we used an ensemble, so performance is not necessarily zero.\n\nResults ::: Rounds become increasingly more difficult.\n\nThis is true for all model architectures.\n\nResults ::: Training on more rounds improves robustness.\n\nGenerally, our results indicate that training on more rounds improves model performance.\n\nThis is true for all model architectures.\n\nResults ::: RoBERTa achieves state-of-the-art performance...\n\nand $90.7$.\n\nResults ::: ...but is outperformed when it is base model.\n\nResults ::: Continuously augmenting training data does not downgrade performance.\n\nFurthermore, as Table TABREF21 shows, training only on ANLI is transferable to SNLI\n\nSNLI and MNLI.\n\nPerformance of such models on ANLI is substantially lower, and decreases with more rounds.\n\nResults ::: Hypothesis-only results ::: RoBERTa does not outperform hypothesis-only on rounds 2 and 3.\n\nThis could mean two things: either the test data is very difficult, or the training data is not good.\n\nHence, this shows that our new challenge test sets are so difficult that the current state-of-the-art model cannot do better than a hypothesis-only prior.\n\nAnalysis\tWe perform two types of model error analysis.\n\n(e.g., SNLI and MNLI).\n\nAnalysis ::: Performance on challenge datasets\tRecently, several hard test sets have been made available for revealing the biases NLI models learn from their training datasets , , , .\n\nWe test our models on these stress tests, after tuning on each test's respective development set to account for potential domain mismatches.\n\nOur results are in Table TABREF23.\n\nWe observe that all of our models far outperform the models\n\n20% in round 2, with coreference featuring prominently.\n\nRelated work ::: Bias in datasets\tMachine learning methods are well-known to pick up on spurious statistical patterns.\n\nFor instance, in image captioning, a simple baseline of utilizing the captions of nearest neighbors in the training set was shown to yield impressive BLEU scores .\n\nThe field\n\nhas a tendency to overfit on static targets, even if that does not happen deliberately .\n\nMany text classification datasets do not require sophisticated linguistic reasoning, as shown by the surprisingly good performance of random encoders .\n\nSimilar observations were made in machine translation  and dialogue .\n\nIn short, the field is rife with dataset bias and papers trying address this important problem.\n\nRelated work ::: Dynamic datasets.\n\nConcurrently with this",
